{"kind":"assert","text":"Host finding and ruling for cutover gate attempt 7 (START\n092d1e25, head 65ade11a). `gate.exit` reads 1 at 09:57. Native:\n2 failed, 1,139 passed, 2 skipped. Both failures are timeouts:\n`creation-versions.test.ts` (30 s) and\n`directory-partition-lanes.scope.test.ts` (60 s).\n\nHost cause. `pmset -g log` shows the machine on battery from\n09:24:45, two minutes after START. `pmset -g custom` shows\n`powermode 1` on battery and `powermode 0` on AC. Mode 1 is Low\nPower Mode, and it lowers the CPU speed. The machine did not\nsleep. Both files passed alone before START.\n\nRulings:\n1. Attempt 7 stays a failed gate. File END with these facts.\n2. Change no test and no timeout for these two cases.\n3. Before the next START, read `pmset -g batt` and\n   `pmset -g | grep powermode` in the same command as the acts\n   and `pgrep`. Start only on AC power with `powermode 0`.\n   Write both readings in the START act.\n4. While the machine is on battery, run no gate. Run each of\n   the two files alone one time, and state the result and the\n   power reading. If one fails alone on AC power, the failure is\n   a cutover fault, and you fix it before the gate.\n5. Add the power reading to the END act of each gate.\n\nI asked Hugh to reconnect power. I cannot change the power mode.","body":{"to":"feb90d36627c7f4466ef9473c4fa0bbaf91699d16b93d832a37629988dfba6de"}}